         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nBefore continuing, we recommend studying these texts:\n\n- **Trigonometry (Sine)**: how the sine connects the angle to the opposite leg, the vertical projection of the radius (<htcite0>)\n- **When size matters**: how to measure an object in one direction with a ruler and compare what we measure (<htcite1>)\n- **Measuring in three dimensions**: how measurements extend to a third direction, which is what gives us three dimensions (<htcite2>)",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "341c9ef9-9320-4c24-8aa4-a533a3290d8a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Atlas",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Sine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "87f5f133-067e-439f-ab1d-eaf45e304079",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "When size matters",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b56ef907-93eb-45ee-ae22-ce4f364ffa4e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Measuring in three dimensions",
                     "date_time": {
