         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"unitaryCircle\" id=\"circtri0\"><style>.circle-draw circle, .circle-draw line {stroke: #71a6d2; stroke-width: 4; stroke-linecap: round; fill: none;} .circle-draw circle { stroke-dasharray: 942; stroke-dashoffset: 942; animation: drawCircle 2s linear forwards;} .radius-line { transform-origin: 300px 170px; animation: rotateRadius 2s linear forwards;} .radius-45 { opacity: 0; animation: showRadius45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .radius-30{ opacity: 0; animation: showRadius30 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .vertical45 { opacity: 0; animation: showVertical45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.5s; } .atlasTextRadius { opacity: 0; animation: atlasTextRadiusMov 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.8s; }</style><style>@keyframes drawCircle { to {stroke-dashoffset: 0; } } @keyframes rotateRadius { from { transform: rotate(0deg); } to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); } } @keyframes showRadius45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showRadius30 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showVertical45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes atlasTextRadiusMov { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }</style><div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 340\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 320 147 A 30 30 0 0 1 340.0 170.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"470\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"8\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"170\" r=\"148\" transform=\"scale(-1 1) translate(-600 0)  rotate(180 300 170)\"></circle> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"448\" y2=\"169\"></line> <line class=\"radius-45\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"66\"></line>  <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"406\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"166\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"188\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">a</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"390\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">b</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"320\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"9\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">y</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"407\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"405\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">Q</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"463\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">x</text></svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Circle with a radius of 1 containing a right triangle.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Where are we on a disk?\n\n#### Walking\n\nWhen we walk down a straight street, we can locate ourselves from a street corner. For example, if the street is divided into blocks and each block is 50 meters, then after walking 4 blocks we have traveled 200 meters from the corner where we started (50 × 4 = 200) (<htcite0>).\n\nThis is a measurement in one dimension: we need only one number — the distance from the corner — to say where we are (<htcite1>).\n\nIn **Graph 1**, the distance between points **O** and **Q** — written **OQ** from now on — is easy to measure precisely because it lies on a straight horizontal line, which we call the **x-axis**.\n\n#### The x-axis\n\nWhen studying the cosine function, we learned that the length of leg **a** equals the radius of the circle multiplied by the cosine of theta (c × cos(θ)). This works because leg **a** is the projection of radius **c** onto the adjacent side (<htcite2>).\n\nIn **Graph 2**, we show that projection again, with an angle theta (θ) between the radius and the **x-axis**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "857f1df2-38ce-4da0-9444-686e16ac2314",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (4 and 5)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7eb77c51-3f08-4c5d-9a87-8c6c5dc0e9d4",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Dimension",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4c95f9bf-befa-42bd-8d56-0cf962e09c14",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Cosine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer1\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 160\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri1\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 150 A 148 148 0 0 0 200 2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 230 130 A 30 30 0 0 1 240.0 150.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"149\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"319\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"67\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"319\" y2=\"67\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"250\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"325\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">Q</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"380\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">x</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"325\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"260\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">a</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"223\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 2</b>: The angle theta (θ) between the radius and the x-axis; the projection of the radius is the segment OQ (leg a).</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The y-axis\n\nLet's reflect before proceeding.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: What about going up?</b><br /><br />Does what we said about a horizontal path apply to a vertical axis?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To answer this reflection, imagine this: you walk 200 meters in one direction — as at the start of this text — then turn onto another street and walk 200 meters more.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: 200 that way, 200 this way...</b><br /><br />Ignore houses, trees, people, and animals, and think only about the straight-line distance of 200 meters.<br /><br />Is the distance you cover the same whether you walk it on the first street or on the second?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "You have probably noticed that the distance of 200 meters is the same on one street as on the other. In both cases we are on a straight line, and no matter which direction we walk, we end up covering those same 200 meters.\n\nNow let's look at the differences. Walking down one straight street, turning onto another, and walking another 200 meters gives two different paths, even though the movement itself is exactly the same in both cases.\n\nSo we use what we learned in the sine text to project the radius of the circle onto the vertical (<htcite0>), as shown in **Graph 3**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "341c9ef9-9320-4c24-8aa4-a533a3290d8a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Sine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 160\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 150 A 148 148 0 0 0 200 2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 230 130 A 30 30 0 0 1 240.0 150.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"149\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"319\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"67\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"319\" y2=\"67\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"250\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"325\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"300\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">b</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"223\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 3</b>: The angle theta (θ) between the hypotenuse and the horizontal, with the opposite leg b (the vertical projection) visible.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In **Graph 3**, leg **b** is highlighted: it is the projection of the radius onto the vertical. The previous text showed that this vertical projection equals the radius of the circle times the sine of theta (c × sin(θ)) (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "341c9ef9-9320-4c24-8aa4-a533a3290d8a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Sine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Axes Together\n\nAs we have seen, the **x-axis** and the **y-axis** are independent of each other: measurements in one direction do not depend on the other. Therefore, to locate point **P** in **Graph 1**, we first say how far we walked from the origin to **Q** on the **x-axis** (**OQ**), and then how far we walked from **Q** to **P** along the **y-axis**.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "A horizontal projection cannot represent the vertical path. The two paths are independent, so we need two measurements — two dimensions (<htcite0>) — to describe the path.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7eb77c51-3f08-4c5d-9a87-8c6c5dc0e9d4",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Dimension",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>P</mn><mi> = </mi><mn>(x, y)</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Representation of the distances walked from the origin <b>O</b> to point <b>P</b>.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Return of the Theorem\n\nThis trigonometry strand of the *History Tracers Atlas* began when we studied how the Babylonians already knew what Dr. Daniel F. Mansfield called the *\"diagonal triple\"* (<htcite0>). Centuries later, this relationship became known as the Pythagorean Theorem, stated in **Formula 2**:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b157e363-321b-4d0e-bf1c-72ac3a0a3f3f",
                     "text": "Mansfield, D. F.",
                     "page": "pp. 985 - 1003",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2021",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7c5ebbe5-75c5-444c-bceb-b0162bff9888",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "History of a Theorem",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 2</b>: Pythagorean Theorem, where <i>a</i> is the adjacent leg, <i>b</i> is the opposite leg, and <i>c</i> is the hypotenuse.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To make what we are studying clearer, let's rename the legs after the paths they measure:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi>(horizontal path)</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><msup><mi>(vertical path)</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><msup><mi>(diagonal path)</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 3</b>: Description of paths.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "As we have seen, the so-called **paths** are projections known as **legs**, while the diagonal is known as the **hypotenuse**. The horizontal leg is projected with the cosine of the angle, and the vertical leg with the sine of the angle (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). We can therefore rewrite **Formula 3** as:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4c95f9bf-befa-42bd-8d56-0cf962e09c14",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Cosine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "341c9ef9-9320-4c24-8aa4-a533a3290d8a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Sine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi>(c × cos (θ))</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><msup><mi>(c × sin (θ))</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><msup><mi>(c)</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi> (θ)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi> (θ)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Pythagorean Theorem written based on the angles and the radius.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Both sides of **Calculation 1** contain the square of the hypotenuse (c&sup2;), so we can divide both sides by it:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi> (θ)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi> (θ)</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mi>c&sup2; cos&sup2; (θ) + c&sup2;sin&sup2; (θ)</mi><mi>c&sup2;</mi></mfrac><mn>=</mn><mfrac><mi>c&sup2;</mi><mi>c&sup2;</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mi>c&sup2; [cos&sup2; (θ) + sin&sup2; (θ)]</mi><mi>c&sup2;</mi></mfrac><mn>=</mn><mfrac><mi>c&sup2;</mi><mi>c&sup2;</mi></mfrac></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1 [cos&sup2; (θ) + sin&sup2; (θ)]</mn><mn>=</mn><mi>1</mi></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>cos&sup2; (θ) + sin&sup2; (θ)</mn><mn>=</mn><mi>1</mi></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Fundamental trigonometric identity.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "**Calculation 2** shows how the lengths and angles around a position on the circle relate to each other:\n- **Line 1**: This line tells us that the square of the horizontal projection of the radius **a** (c&sup2; cos&sup2; (θ)), added to the square of its vertical projection **b** (c&sup2; sin&sup2; (θ)), equals the square of the total length of the radius (c&sup2;), also known as the **hypotenuse**;\n- **Line 2**: Since the sum of the squares of the projections equals the square of the radius itself, we express each part as a fraction of the whole, with the total (c&sup2;) in the denominator;\n- **Line 3**: Since both projections share the radius **c**, we factor it out, separating it from the parts that depend on the angle;\n- **Line 4**: The common factor cancels out — c&sup2; / c&sup2; equals 1 — so only the terms that depend on the angle remain;\n- **Line 5**: As a consequence, the square of the cosine of θ plus the square of the sine of θ equals 1.\n\n#### The Location on the Circle\n\nWith this information, we can rewrite **Formula 1** in several equivalent forms, each describing our exact position at point **P** on the circle. **Graph 4** and **Formula 4** present two of them:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 160\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 150 A 148 148 0 0 0 200 2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 200 120 A 30 30 0 0 1 230.0 150.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"4\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"15\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"208\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 4</b>: The radius pointing straight up: the angle theta (θ) is 90°.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
