            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>P</mn><mi>=</mi><mn>(x, y)</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>P</mn><mi>=</mi><mn>(a, b)</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>P</mn><mi>=</mi><mn>(c × cos (θ), c × sin (θ))</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 4</b>: Representation of the distances walked from the origin <b>O</b> to point <b>P</b>.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From **Formula 4**, we conclude that we can describe our position on a circle in the following ways:\n- **Line 1**: Point **P** is at a distance **x** from the origin **O** and at a distance **y** from that same origin **O**.\n- **Line 2**: The distance **x** corresponds to the length of leg **a**, which projects the hypotenuse **c** onto the horizontal, while the distance **y** corresponds to the length of leg **b**, which projects the hypotenuse **c** onto the vertical;\n- **Line 3**: Each projection can be expressed with the radius **c** — the distance from the origin — and the angle θ — the opening of the radius from the horizontal, measured counterclockwise. Thus the horizontal path is <b>x = a = c × cos (θ)</b>, and the vertical path is <b>y = b = c × sin (θ)</b>.\n\nRegardless of the form chosen to express the position, we can state that the path walked at the start of this text is given by:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>P</mn><mi>=</mi><mn>(200, 200)</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 5</b>: Representation of the distances traveled from the origin <b>O</b> to point <b>P</b>.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we saw different ways to express a path we have walked. Whichever form we choose, the horizontal and vertical displacements are measured independently, and together they account for the whole walk — the 200 + 200 of our example ends at P = (200, 200), as in **Formula 5**.\n\nWe also saw, through the Pythagorean Theorem, that the relationship between the projections of the hypotenuse onto its legs can be described with the cosine and sine of the angle between the hypotenuse and the horizontal.",
